      <h1><span class="chrome-text">Why would you scan a spaceborne antenna from above?</span></h1>
      <p class="post-subtitle">NASA's 10 m x 10 m horizontal near-field range</p>
      <div class="post-byline"><span><strong>Dom Belgiovane, PhD</strong></span><span>Tuesday, October 27, 2026</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="post container-narrow">
    <div class="measure">
      <p>Why would you hang a near-field probe from the ceiling and point the antenna up?</p>
      <p>Most planar near-field ranges you have seen are vertical. The antenna sits on its side and the probe moves up and down a wall. That works well for a lot of hardware. It works less well for a large deployable reflector designed to fly in zero gravity, because on its side it sags, and you end up measuring a shape it will never have in orbit.</p>
      <p>That was the starting point for the facility in our 2025 AMTA paper, "NASA's Horizontal Planar Near-Field Facility: A Large-Scale, High-Accuracy System for Spaceborne Antenna Testing," with Jim Downey, Bryan Schoenholtz and Félix Miranda of NASA Glenn Research Center, and Evan Beers, James Caputo and me, then at MVG-OATI. An earlier version was presented at the AMTA Regional Meeting in Tempe, AZ on May 20, 2025.</p>

      <h2>The requirement: big, broadband, and accurate</h2>
      <p>NASA commissioned a horizontal planar near-field (HPNF) range for space communications antennas, including active electronically scanned arrays and parabolic reflectors. The headline numbers:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>10 m x 10 m scan plane, with 1.5 m of Z travel</li>
        <li>1 to 110 GHz</li>
        <li>Gain, patterns in any linear polarization, beam pointing, sidelobes, plus active test modes</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Each of those on its own is manageable. Together, they make the RF subsystem and the positioner accuracy the hard parts. Accuracy that is fine at 1 GHz is not fine at 110 GHz, and a 10 m span is a lot of distance to hold flat.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="wide" style="max-width:1880px">
