    <div class="measure">
      <h2>Holding 100 square meters flat</h2>
      <p>The scanner uses two X-axis linear slides on six floor-mounted masts, leveled as one unit, with a Y-axis beam riding on top that carries the Z and roll stages. The probe aperture sits roughly 7.6 to 9.1 m above the floor.</p>
      <p>Corrected, each axis is specified to ±0.06 mm, with planarity of 0.035 mm RMS over the full scan area. The word "corrected" is doing the work in that sentence. A laser tracker was used for initial alignment and positioner corrections: X and Y position as a function of (X, Y), Z planarity as a function of (X, Y), and the roll axis aligned to the scan plane. The tracker was also used to measure DUT rotation and translation errors at mounting.</p>
      <p>The DUT side is just as practical. A telescoping mast (3 to 6 m) sits on a 2 m x 2 m cart with a hexapod on top for fine alignment. The cart is pushed in by hand over locating features, with about 0.02 mm repeatability, so large antennas can be rolled in instead of craned onto a tower. Pull the mast and you can put heavy payloads, or even a vehicle such as a lunar rover or a UAV, on the floor for upper-hemisphere measurements.</p>

      <h2>One RF system, 1 to 110 GHz</h2>
      <p>Below 50 GHz, the system runs a dual-PXIe architecture: one chassis on the scanner and one at the DUT, each with Keysight PXIe VNA modules, direct RF from 1 to 50 GHz. Shielded enclosures on the Z axis and DUT tower down-convert test and reference signals to IF, with a multiplier used above 20 GHz. A shared LO from a source in a ground rack keeps the two sides phase coherent.</p>
      <p>From 50 to 110 GHz, VDI frequency extender modules cover V, E and W bands. Below 50 GHz the probes are dual polarized; in the mmWave bands, polarization is changed by rotating the probe roll axis.</p>
      <p>Calibration traceability was built in from the start. Every probe came with CST-generated pattern correction coefficients and a gain calibration at UPM. Every reference standard gain horn came with an on-axis gain calibration at NPL.</p>
      <p>The near-field dynamic range requirement was more than 60 dB. Measured over the air at boresight (50 sweeps, 1 kHz IFBW), it cleared that requirement, with a stepped attenuator used to confirm linearity.</p>
    </div>

