    <div class="measure">
      <h2>More than patterns</h2>
      <p>Because the payloads include active antennas, the range supports special test modes beyond gain and patterns:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>EIRP:</strong> NF-to-FF transform for gain, with input power from the PXI in spectrum analyzer mode or a power meter through a calibrated coupler path</li>
        <li><strong>G/T:</strong> an over-the-air procedure using the near-field peak location, probe-side power meter and DUT-side noise measurements</li>
        <li><strong>IMD:</strong> delta IM3, either swept frequency with fixed delta, or swept delta at a fixed center frequency</li>
        <li><strong>Gain compression (P1dB):</strong> automated input power sweeps per frequency</li>
        <li><strong>Pulsed RF:</strong> using the PXI internal pulsing hardware, or an external pulse switch</li>
        <li><strong>Park and probe:</strong> the probe parks over an element of an active array and cycles through phase shifter or attenuator states, then moves to the next element</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Does the uncertainty budget match reality?</h2>
      <p>This is the part I would point any range engineer to.</p>
      <p>We ran an uncertainty analysis based on the NIST 18-term near-field error model and IEEE Std 1720, computed inside the valid angle, at frequencies across the bands. Each term was evaluated by analysis or measurement: probe calibration data from UPM, laser tracker data used to perturb ideal data and push it through the transform, quarter-wavelength probe and DUT offsets for multiple reflections and room scattering, boresight roll and attenuator checks for linearity, drift tests, and back-to-back repeatability. Main beam 2σ uncertainty landed in the range of about ±0.23 to ±0.52 dB depending on band.</p>
      <p>Then we checked it against measurement. At each band, five measurements were taken at 1/8-wavelength Z steps. The mean of the five became the "truth," and the 3σ spread within the 3 dB main beam became the gain accuracy metric. The idea is simple: a 3σ (99.7%) spread from real data should fall inside the 2σ (95.4%) interval from the analysis. Measured main-beam 3σ values came in at roughly ±0.09 to ±0.35 dB, and in every band where both numbers were reported, the measured 3σ sat inside the analyzed 2σ. [CHECK: numbers are from the May 2025 NTRS slides; confirm they match the final symposium paper.]</p>
    </div>

